# TEMPLATE — copy to deploy/.env on the box, fill in, `chmod 600`, root-owned.
# deploy/.env is gitignored (the repo-root .gitignore matches `.env` unanchored).
# Every UNCOMMENTED key below is a hard `:?` requirement in docker-compose.yml: compose
# refuses to start without it rather than starting with a default nobody chose. The
# commented-out keys are the exception and are optional by construction — ACME_CA,
# ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE, IMAGE_TAG and the EMAIL_* block each carry a default in the compose
# file. Where a default exists it is stated beside the key, because "optional" without the
# resulting behaviour written down is how a value nobody chose becomes production.
#
# NOT selected here: COMPOSE_FILE. The stack is always selected by path
# (`docker compose -f /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/docker-compose.yml ...`) because
# COMPOSE_FILE in a project's .env overrides file resolution and the compose guard
# is structurally blind to it (#1217).

# --- Edge -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname the certificate is issued for. K1: dev.jobbliggaren.se first; the
# apex stays parked at Strato.
SITE_HOST=dev.jobbliggaren.se

# Contact address Let's Encrypt uses for expiry warnings.
#
# MUST be the controller's own address. Never a shared or user-facing mailbox: with Cloudflare
# gone (Klas decision K3, ADR 0050 `Amendment 2026-08-04` §3), Caddy talks to Let's Encrypt
# directly, so ISRG (USA) is the only new party the edge gained. The controller's own address is
# the controller's own data and owes no processor entry -- a user-facing address here would make
# ISRG a recipient of user data and reopen Chapter V. This line prescribes; it does not measure.
# The value lives only on the box, and #1199 could not read it (security-auditor 2026-08-09).
ACME_EMAIL=

# Leave unset for production issuance. Set to
# https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory while proving the challenge
# path — production limits are 5 duplicate certificates per week and 5 failed
# validations per hour, and a shadowed challenge burns them.
#ACME_CA=

# Which ACME challenge type Caddy is allowed to use. Leave unset: the default `both` resolves
# to an EMPTY snippet, i.e. Caddy's own behaviour with both challenges enabled.
#
# The two other values exist to PROVE issuance, never to run on. Caddy runs HTTP-01 and
# TLS-ALPN-01 and chooses between them adaptively, so a certificate obtained with both enabled
# says nothing about which path carried it — and the runbook is blunt about the consequence:
# "Never configure TLS-ALPN-01 away without having proven HTTP-01 for real." Force one at a
# time, on the STAGING directory, and read the issuance log line that names the challenge type.
#
#   ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=http01   # TLS-ALPN-01 disabled — verification-log row 5
#   ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=alpn01   # HTTP-01 disabled    — verification-log row 6
#
# THE KEY IS PERMANENT, and a follow-up that deletes it after cutover would be a defect
# (CTO 2026-08-05). The certificate renews forever, and the renewal path can be broken later
# by a firewall change, a Caddy upgrade or a DNS move. This is a diagnostic instrument; without
# it the next incident starts by re-deriving and re-deploying the mechanism on a box that is
# down at that moment.
#ACME_CHALLENGE_MODE=both

# K2 admission gate. Generate the hash with (one line):
#
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext '<password>' --algorithm bcrypt --bcrypt-cost 11
#
# COST 11, NOT THE DEFAULT 14, and it is a decision rather than a shortcut (Klas
# 2026-08-05). Caddy caches only SUCCESSFUL comparisons, so a wrong password pays the
# full hash every time, which makes this gate an unauthenticated CPU amplifier against a
# box with no CDN in front of it. Cost 14 defends against offline cracking of a leaked
# hash; YOUR hash is never published. It lives in `deploy/.env` on the box and, measured,
# a second time in Docker's own container state — both root-only, and anyone who can read
# either already has root. The default bought a threat we do not have and paid for
# it in availability we do need.
#
# ARGON2ID IS REFUSED, and it is refused at the CLASS rather than at a threshold, even
# though Caddy calls it recommended and bcrypt legacy. Its default is m=47104, i.e. 46
# MiB allocated PER VERIFICATION. Any per-request memory allocation on an unfiltered,
# unauthenticated edge is a memory-exhaustion primitive, and the attacker picks the
# concurrency — so no value of m makes it safe here, and lowering m does not rescue it.
# Raising the edge cap would be the wrong direction entirely: it admits the primitive
# and then feeds it.
#
# WRAP THE HASH IN SINGLE QUOTES. Store the plaintext in a password manager, never here.
# The reason matters because the damage does not look like damage: compose interpolates
# `.env`, and where a `$` is followed by a character that can START a variable name it
# eats the longest run of [A-Za-z0-9_] and substitutes it away. That is why `$2a` and
# `$11` survive - a name cannot start with a digit - while a bcrypt salt beginning with a
# LETTER loses its leading run up to the first `.` or `/`.
#
# IT IS THE COMMON CASE, NOT AN EDGE CASE. The bcrypt alphabet is `./A-Za-z0-9`, so 52 of
# 64 possible first salt characters are letters - about four in five hashes are mangled.
# Measured on Compose 2.40.3, 2026-08-05, over 20 real cost-11 hashes: 16 mangled, 4
# intact. The version belongs to the claim: interpolation semantics are what this is about.
#
# The mangled form is the danger, and it is worth seeing once. This pair is reproducible:
# the plaintext is the literal string `losenord-6`, generated for this measurement and
# used for nothing, so the hash below is worth exactly nothing to anyone who cracks it.
#
#   in   $2a$11$Yq74328Mrsj/hVw45XkEi.37o3Y.rENU2ySYye1b2bLA/jpzdZ7i6
#   out  $2a$11/hVw45XkEi.37o3Y.rENU2ySYye1b2bLA/jpzdZ7i6
#
# Note what the output is NOT: it is not a short, obviously broken `$2a$11`. It is long
# and plausible, so an operator eyeballing it sees a hash. Compose warns and exits 0,
# `:?` does not fire, and the site then 401s on the correct password. Single quotes stop
# interpolation and make the outcome the same every time.
#
# YOUR hash never goes in this file. This repo is public.
BASIC_AUTH_USER=
BASIC_AUTH_HASH=''

# --- Application secrets: NOT IN THIS FILE ----------------------------------
# The field-encryption master key and the three pseudonymisation peppers were removed
# from here by #198 (ADR 0050 gate B-1: the master key must never be plaintext on disk).
# They are written to /run/jobbliggaren/secrets/ — tmpfs, RAM-backed — by the operator
# after every boot, and reach api and worker as a read-only bind mount. There is no
# encrypted-at-rest copy on this disk, deliberately; the host has no TPM, so a sealed
# blob and its unsealing key would travel together in any disk snapshot.
#
# Procedure, including the per-boot re-injection: docs/runbooks/master-key-ops.md.
#
# ROTATABILITY IS PER SECRET, AND THE OLD ONE-SENTENCE RULE HERE WAS WRONG. This block used
# to say "GENERATE ONCE, then never rotate" about all four values at once. That is a template
# sentence covering four different truths, and it was taken for a measurement — it is what
# led an earlier design to move the peppers byte-identically when three of the four could
# simply be replaced. Per secret:
#
#   FieldEncryption master key — ROTATABLE. Re-wrapping the stored per-user DEKs is a bounded
#     operation (#198 gate M-3); field data is never touched. The runnable arm
#     (`migrate rewrap-master-key`) ships in #198's second PR and is NOT yet code. Note that
#     with zero rows in user_data_keys there is nothing to re-wrap at all, so a rotation today
#     is simply new bytes.
#
#   AuditPseudonymization pepper — ROTATABLE AT ANY TIME, including against a full database.
#     The HMAC is written into a write-only jsonb audit field that no read path ever matches
#     against, and the partitions age out. The DI registration already calls it
#     "rotation-tolerant".
#
#   CvReviewFingerprintPseudonymization pepper — rotatable while `resume_finding_statuses` is
#     EMPTY. The fingerprint is recomputable, but rotating it makes every stored
#     target_fingerprint mismatch, so every finding a user marked Ignored or Resolved silently
#     reverts to Open.
#
#   CompanyWatchPseudonymization pepper — ROTATABLE ONLY WHILE `company_watches` HAS NO ROWS,
#     and the window closes at the FIRST row, not at "first real data". The backfill destroyed
#     the plaintext organisation number in place, so tokens cannot be recomputed under a new
#     pepper — not expensively, but mathematically. One test user following one company locks
#     this pepper permanently.
#
# Losing any of the four has the same effect as rotating it after that point: the data it
# protects becomes unreadable or unmatchable. Escrow accordingly.

# --- Database roles ---------------------------------------------------------
# Operator-provisioned; `migrate init` creates the roles with these passwords and
# the application then connects as jobbliggaren_app / jobbliggaren_worker.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
POSTGRES_MASTER_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_MIGRATIONS_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_WORKER_PASSWORD=

# --- Release ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The rollback control, and it rolls back CODE ONLY. Unset means `latest`. To roll
# back, pin the previous published tag (`sha-<short>`) and re-run the reconcile unit
# — seconds, and it is the primary rollback path for api, worker, web and caddy.
#
# IT IS NOT A SCHEMA ROLLBACK. `migrate` runs EF migrations before api and worker
# start, so pinning an older tag re-runs an older migrate against a database a newer
# one already changed. There are no down-migrations, and this repo has measured cases
# where that direction destroys data irreversibly. Nothing here stops it yet — #1236
# owns the control. Across a migration boundary, treat a backwards pin as a restore
# problem, not a deploy problem.
#
# A Netcup snapshot is NOT deploy rollback; its role is before a migration, once real
# user data exists — which is exactly the boundary the tag cannot cross.
#IMAGE_TAG=sha-0000000

# --- Outbound email (#183) --------------------------------------------------
# ALL OPTIONAL, AND THE DEFAULTS ARE THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Leaving this whole block
# commented out yields Email__Provider=Console, which outside Development/Test resolves to
# NullEmailSender: the application finds its recipients, builds its messages, and delivers
# them nowhere, silently. That is what the box does today and this block does not change it.
#
# SETTING EMAIL_PROVIDER=Ses IS THE PROD FLIP. It is gated by
# docs/runbooks/release-checklist.md §2.5 — point 1 is not green, and its legs' statuses are
# read in the point itself, never from a summary — and ADR 0124 states that CC never
# performs it. Do not uncomment it to "test something".
#
# Setup procedure for everything below, including the DNS half: docs/runbooks/email-ses-setup.md.
#EMAIL_PROVIDER=Ses
#
# Always explicit. The SDK's default region chain (AWS_REGION / ~/.aws/config / IMDS) must
# never decide which jurisdiction mail leaves from — AddEmailSender refuses to register
# without this value when the provider is Ses.
#EMAIL_SES_REGION=eu-north-1
#
# Pointers to files, NOT the credentials themselves — the `_FILE` seam, same as the master
# key. A pointer naming a path that cannot be read THROWS at boot, so leave these commented
# until the files exist.
#
# NOTHING WRITES THESE FILES YET, and that is the honest state rather than an oversight:
# jobbliggaren-inject-secrets.sh carries a fixed SECRET_KEYS array and is fail-loud, so
# adding SES to it would demand the credentials at every boot and take the box down before
# there is any use for them. Extending the injection is part of the flip.
#EMAIL_SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID_FILE=/run/app-secrets/Email__Ses__AccessKeyId
#EMAIL_SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=/run/app-secrets/Email__Ses__SecretAccessKey
#
# EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS is deliberately NOT a variable: the sender must lie under the
# SES-verified domain identity, and p=reject makes a wrong sender silence rather than a
# bounce — so the compose file carries the literal, and there is no knob whose only
# reachable non-default values are wrong ones.
#
# Email__BaseUrl is NOT a variable here either: compose derives it from SITE_HOST, because
# the links in confirmation mail must point at this box and the code default is
# http://localhost:3000.

# ==================================================================================
# Structured log sink — #1175 (B), Seq on the internal network
# ==================================================================================
#
# UNSET LEAVES BOTH HOSTS CONSOLE-ONLY, EXACTLY AS BEFORE. AddJobbliggarenLogging attaches
# the Seq provider only when `Seq:ServerUrl` is non-empty, so an absent value here is not a
# half-configured sink — it is the pre-#1175 behaviour, unchanged.
#
# THE PORT IS THE INGESTION LISTENER, NOT THE UI. Seq serves its UI and query API on 80 and
# accepts ingestion on 5341. Pointing the app at 5341 means a compromised api or worker can
# write events and cannot read them back over the network.
#
# THE THREE KEYS HAVE AN ORDER, AND THIS ONE IS LAST — which is why it ships commented out while
# the other two ship empty. Setting it is what ATTACHES the provider, so a fresh copy with this
# active and the other two empty points both hosts at a Seq that refuses to start (no admin
# password) with no ingest key to authenticate with. Order: SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD, then seq up, then
# the ingest key created inside Seq, then this. log-sink.md §3 is the procedure.
#SEQ_SERVER_URL=http://seq:5341
#
# An INGEST-ONLY API key, created in Seq after first run (Settings -> API Keys). Not a
# root-survival control — root on this box reads the volume directly — but it stops a
# compromised application container from reading or deleting the corpus.
SEQ_INGEST_API_KEY=
#
# Read ONLY on Seq's first run against an empty volume. After that the password lives in
# Seq's own store and THIS VALUE STOPS BEING THE SOURCE OF TRUTH — change it in Seq, not
# here. Seq 2026.1 refuses to start without it (measured 2026-08-11), which is the
# fail-closed default that makes #1198's unauthenticated-for-months shape hard to recreate.
SEQ_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
#
# Optional. Defaults to Information in the compose file.
#SEQ_MINIMUM_LEVEL=Information
